"""VO beats for How to Buy a TBM 930 (Ep6, N24MY, 2016 Daher TBM 930).
Story cut (Ep3 v2 / Ep4 / Ep5 shape): cold open + title -> choose -> ladder -> shortlist -> the one we ran -> report sections as questions -> offer -> keep watching -> verdict.
Plain-language standard (Alex 2026-10-09): every number gets the pattern, a "because" that names a person, and the so-what.
Numbers as words, no ellipses, sentences of about 20 words or fewer."""
BEATS = [
("b01", "title", "This airplane's engine is about five hundred seventy hours younger than the airplane itself. That one number changes the offer. This is How to Buy a TBM nine thirty. One real airplane for sale right now, one real NextPlane report, and the questions a careful buyer asks."),
("b02", "problem", "Step one. Choose which one. The TBM is a single-engine turboprop: one turbine engine, burning jet fuel, turning a propeller. It is pressurized, seats six, and cruises at about three hundred thirty knots. This week, NextPlane is tracking forty-four TBMs for sale in the United States. Twenty-six show a price."),
("b03", "ladder", "The first question is which generation, because the generation sets the price. We use the typical asking price: half ask more, half ask less. The original TBM seven hundreds, built through two thousand five, ask about one point seven million dollars. The TBM eight fifties, about two point four five million. The nine hundred series, built from twenty fourteen to twenty nineteen, about three point three million. The newer nine forties ask three point seven to four point two million. And the latest nine sixties, about four point nine to five point two million. Only two of each show a price, so treat those as examples."),
("b04", "shortlist", "Now narrow it down. Say you want the nine thirty, with Garmin's G three thousand touchscreen cockpit, at a used price. Four twenty sixteen nine thirties are for sale, and three show a price. Three point two nine five million, three point three four nine million, and three point four million. So we ran the cheapest, to see why it is cheapest."),
("b05", "pick", "This one. A twenty sixteen nine thirty, listed by a California dealer, asking about three point three million dollars. Here is what stopped us. The airframe shows twenty-two hundred eighteen hours. The engine shows sixteen hundred fifty hours, since new. So this engine, most likely, has not been in this airplane its whole life. And a big engine inspection is close."),
("b06", "header", "Step two. Evaluate the one. Open the NextPlane Aircraft Report. The top section is the summary, with warnings and good news in one place. No accident record. No loan recorded against it in the last year. The same company has owned it for about three and a half years. But first, is the price fair?"),
("b07", "value", "This section is the valuation, NextPlane's estimate of what a similar airplane is worth today, built from comparable listings. The estimate is about three point four four million dollars. But we checked its six listings. Three are no longer for sale. Using the five similar airplanes for sale today, the typical ask is about three point three five million. This one is fifty-four thousand below that. So it is not a big discount. It is the low end of the market, and the hours explain why."),
("b08", "hours", "This section compares the airplane's hours with the market. The airframe has twenty-two hundred eighteen hours. The other two priced twenty sixteen nine thirties have eighteen hundred seventy and eighteen hundred. More hours usually means a lower price, because buyers pay for time left, not time used."),
("b09", "engine", "This section is the maintenance program, the inspections coming due. One matters most: the hot section inspection. That is a mid-life check of the hottest parts inside a turbine engine. The report schedules it every eighteen hundred hours. This engine shows sixteen hundred fifty. So about a hundred fifty hours remain. The nearest rival's listing says it just came out of a recent hot section inspection. It asks fifty-four thousand dollars more. Our data has no reliable cost for it. So get a quote from a TBM shop before you offer."),
("b10", "pricehist", "This section is the listing history, every price change NextPlane recorded. Has the seller moved? Once. Listed on August thirteenth at three point three nine five million dollars. Cut by one hundred thousand on September ninth. Nothing since. Of twenty-six TBMs listed this spring and early summer, only four cut their price. The typical cut was about two percent. More than half were still listed after ninety days. TBM owners often are not forced to sell, so they wait. This seller has already moved more than most."),
("b11", "owner", "Who owns it? This section covers ownership: the registered owner, how long they have held it, and any recorded loans. The owner is a Texas company, registered since March twenty twenty-three. A California dealer lists it. No loan is recorded on the airframe. But an engine can carry its own loan. The report asks for the engine serial number to check that. Get it from the engine logbook. Then run a title search, and pay through escrow, a neutral party that holds the money."),
("b12", "flight", "Is it flying? This section shows how much the airplane really flies, from NextPlane's flight data. Over two years, we see about four hundred thirty-five hours. In the last twelve months, about two hundred fifty-six. In the last three months, about sixty-five, while it has been for sale. Its most frequent trip is Bakersfield, California to Odessa, Texas. So the hours in the listing are already out of date. At this pace, the hot section inspection could arrive in about seven months. Ask for current hours, in writing."),
("b13", "ads", "What should the logbooks show? This section lists mandatory safety fixes, called airworthiness directives. The FAA treats the nine thirty as a version of the TBM seven hundred. So older directives can apply. The report matches seventeen to this airplane. Six only apply if certain parts are installed. Several repeat every hundred hours. A twenty twenty-four emergency directive covers turbine blades in this engine model. Whether it applies depends on the engine serial number. With an engine that is not the original, check this one first."),
("b14", "sdr", "Has it ever been hurt? The listing reports no damage. NextPlane finds no accident record. Reported service problems: only two on this model in five years, and none on this airplane. A clean record."),
("b15", "opcost", "What will it cost to keep? This section estimates a year of ownership. At two hundred hours a year, about one hundred seventy-two thousand dollars, or eight hundred sixty-one an hour. Fuel is the biggest line, about sixty-one thousand. The fuel figure is an estimate from engine power, so ask the seller for real fuel records."),
("b16", "msrp", "What did it cost new? This section shows the original price. Typically equipped, about four point two million dollars in twenty sixteen. So the ask is about twenty-two percent below new, after ten years. A new nine sixty cost about five and a half million in twenty twenty-five. Rising new prices tend to hold up used ones, because buyers priced out of new move to used."),
("b17", "seller", "And who am I dealing with? A dealer in California, selling an airplane registered to a Texas company. So ask one question early: does the dealer own the airplane, or sell it for the owner? Then book an independent pre-buy inspection, at a TBM shop you choose."),
("b18", "offer", "Step three. Price the offer. Similar airplanes for sale today ask a typical three point three five million. This one asks less, but has the most hours, and its hot section inspection is close. The rival with the fresh inspection just cut fifty thousand. This seller has moved once, by a hundred thousand. NextPlane's model gives about a one in four chance of another cut within ninety days. So a reasonable opening offer is around three point two million dollars, about three percent under the ask. Make it subject to the pre-buy, current hours, and the engine logbook. Ask the seller to do the hot section inspection before closing, or take its quoted cost off the price."),
("b19", "watch", "Step four. Keep watching. In the last two weeks, five TBMs were newly listed. Sellers made three price cuts, and no increases. One cut was this airplane's closest rival. Right now, sellers are moving, slowly."),
("b20", "verdict", "So who is this airplane right for? A buyer who flies a lot, and wants the nine thirty at the lowest ask. Someone ready to pay for the hot section inspection soon. It is not right for you if you want low hours, or no engine work in your first year. Fairly priced at the low end, with a clean history. Check three things: why the engine is younger than the airframe, when the inspection is due, and the current hours."),
("b21", "end", "Before you call the broker, check the data at NextPlane dot U S."),
]
if __name__ == "__main__":
    import re
    n = sum(len(t.split()) for _, _, t in BEATS); print("words", n)
    assert not any("..." in t for _, _, t in BEATS)
    for b, _, t in BEATS:
        for s in re.split(r"(?<=[.?!])\s+", t):
            if len(s.split()) > 22: print("LONG", b, len(s.split()), s)
